    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change works additively, but two structural issues make it diverge from this codebase's own conventions.\n\n**1. `customerCode` format knowledge is split across units \u2014 against the established `IdentityKey` pattern.**\nThe `<REGION>-<HASH8>` format is now a real structured identity: it is *built* in `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` (`region + \"-\" + hash8`) but *parsed* in `Localities.regionOf` (`indexOf('-')` / `substring`). Those two places must agree on the separator and layout, yet neither owns the format.\n\nThis codebase already has the right idiom for exactly this situation \u2014 `IdentityKey` co-locates construction (`of`) and read-back (`ofOwner`) of a derived key, with a javadoc rationale: \"so both compute it the same way and cannot drift apart.\" The customerCode redesign should follow it: introduce a small `CustomerCode` helper (in `rest.function.owner`, next to `IdentityKey`) that owns both `format(region, hash8)` and `regionOf(code)`. `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` and `OwnerMapper` would each delegate, and the format lives in one place.\n\nPutting `regionOf(customerCode)` in `Localities` also leaks the customerCode format into a class whose stated job is city/postcode\u2192region derivation \u2014 it now knows how customer codes are structured, which isn't its concern.\n\n**2. `Localities.of(String city, String postcode)` is now dead, and overlaps `ofPostcode`.**\nThe mapper was its only caller; that mapping now uses `regionOf`. The two-arg `of` has no remaining callers in `src` (verified). Meanwhile the new `ofPostcode` is essentially `of(city, postcode)` minus the city fallback \u2014 both wrap the same `regionForPostcode` core. The spec says locality derivation \"must all move\" to the new identity, so the old postcode-preferring path should be removed rather than left as a fourth, unused, near-duplicate region method on the class. Delete `of(String, String)` (keep single-arg `of` \u2014 still used by `postcodeRange`).\n\nEverything else is clean: `Hashes` is a well-scoped, well-justified new utility (private ctor, single responsibility, the OfficeFloor one-public-method rationale is apt); dropping the `OwnerRepository` dependency from `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` is a genuine simplification; the wiring (`NormalizeOwnerTelephone` before `customerCode`) and the `@link` in the javadoc are valid; and the openapi/mapper updates are consistent with the new identity.",
